# Optional overrides. Both the Python CLI and the PowerShell scripts load `.env`
# from the repository root automatically; a real environment variable always wins.
# The doctor can normally discover a standard Steam install.
STS2_GAME_ROOT=C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\Slay the Spire 2
STS2_ASSEMBLY=C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\Slay the Spire 2\data_sts2_windows_x86_64\sts2.dll
GODOT=C:\path\to\Godot_v4.5.1-stable_mono_win64.exe
DIVINE_STS2_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=60

# Sandboxed sessions can only write inside the checkout, while the Godot host needs a
# writable `user://` directory (without one the engine aborts before serving a request)
# and the .NET CLI needs a writable home for its first-run sentinels. Point both at
# ignored directories inside the checkout:
#
#   APPDATA=<checkout>\artifacts\godot-appdata
#   LOCALAPPDATA=<checkout>\artifacts\godot-localappdata
#   DOTNET_CLI_HOME=<checkout>\.tools\dotnet-home
#   NUGET_PACKAGES=<checkout>\.tools\nuget-packages
#
# APPDATA and LOCALAPPDATA are the only documented exception to "a real environment
# variable wins" (`ENV_FILE_OVERRIDE_KEYS` in `python/sts2_native_sim/paths.py`, mirrored
# in `scripts/common.ps1`): Windows always defines them, so without the exception neither
# could be redirected from this file. Relocating LOCALAPPDATA also relocates the
# full-application sandbox root (`<LOCALAPPDATA>\divine-sts2\full-app-sandboxes`).
#
# Only processes that load this file inherit these values: the Python entry points
# (`find_godot()`/`find_game_root()` trigger the load) and PowerShell scripts that
# dot-source `scripts/common.ps1`. A bare `dotnet build` in a sandboxed shell therefore
# needs these exported first, including the two MSBuild switches a restricted sandbox
# requires (MSBuild imports environment variables as properties):
#
#   BuildInParallel=false  out-of-proc build nodes need named pipes the sandbox blocks, so
#                          parallel project-reference builds fail with "0 errors"; keep it
#                          unset when you want parallel builds.
#   NuGetAudit=false       unreachable nuget.org turns the package-vulnerability audit into
#                          NU1900, which TreatWarningsAsErrors escalates for projects with a
#                          PackageReference; omit it when nuget.org is reachable.
